{{Politics of Balisca}}
The '''Baliscan confederal budget''' ({{lang-bl|presupuesto confederal}}) refers to the annual revenue (money received) and expenditures (money spent) of the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]]. As budget expenditures are issued on a yearly basis by the [[Confederal Congress]], they reflect the country's {{W|fiscal policy}}.

The budget principles are defined by the [[Constitution of Balisca|Baliscan Constitution]] and have been restated most recently in the 1999 fiscal guidelines of the confederation. In 2020 the confederal budget was {{Abbr|433|433093051574}} billion USD, which was around 7% of the country's [[W:GDP|GDP]] that year. In 2025 the confederal budget of Balisca was {{Abbr|729|729,503,007,348}} billion USD, or 10.79% of the country's GDP.

Note that the Baliscan ''confederal'' budget only comprises 28.5% of Baliscan public expenditure, the rest being managed by the [[Cantons of Balisca|cantons]] and the [[Communes of Balisca|communes]] directly.

==General overview==
As the confederal system in Balisca divides administration into three levels; confederation, cantons and communes, the confederal budget refers solely to the revenues and expenditures at the national level. The cantonal budgets, as well as the budgets of the more than 57,681 communes are not within the competence of the [[Politics of Balisca|confederal government]] or [[Confederal Congress|Congress]]. Their revenues and expenditures are therefore not counted as part of the confederal budget, but they together amount to more than 60% of total public spending. 

However, the different budget levels are fiscally linked together. There are political instruments as for instance the "new fiscal harmonization"-law, which regulate financial payments from the confederal government to the cantons and communes as well as from the fiscally more to the fiscally less potent cantons.

Within these important frameworks many principles of distribution of money are regulated, as for example how different projects can be realized, using joint funds from communes, cantons and the confederation alike. In this sense, the different budget levels are fiscally interconnected, but politically separated from each other.

